## Lesson: ratio-cocycle-obstruction
Lesson ID: BL-20261003-ratio-cocycle-obstruction
Scope: mathematical problem-solving / adapted-structure existence questions
Problem Description: Attempts to prove existence of an almost complex structure adapted to a geometric configuration (e.g. J-holomorphic local defining functions for a divisor) kept failing at the gluing step: different local adapted models disagreed on overlaps. Trigger condition: a construction requires locally defined "adapted" objectss whose transition data on overlaps would inherit a holomorphicity-type rigidity.
Root Cause: The ratio of two holomorphic defining functions of the same hypersurface is itself holomorphic (CR identity on the dense open complement of the divisor, extended across by continuity); on a compact connected stratum the maximum principle forces it constant; hence transition cocycles are locally constant, the (co)normal bundle becomes flat, and its Euler class must vanish. Existence questions of this type are therefore often NO for topological reasons, before any analysis.
Solution: Test overlap behaviour first by computing the $\bar\partial$ of the transition ratio of two candidate adapted objects; if it vanishes, derive the flatness/Euler-class obstruction and look for a counterexample (for SNC-type questions: $(\CP^2, \text{line})$, self-intersection 1). This converts an unmanageable YES-construction into a short rigorous disproof.
Constraints: Applies when the adapted objects are holomorphic in overlapping senses and the underlying strata are compact; obstruction is characteristic-class level (real coefficients; torsion may survive).
Validation Evidence: prob-004 round 0: full disproof written in solution.tex (Theorem 1.1 obstruction + Corollary 1.2 counterexample), compiled and audited; the same computation blocked three earlier YES-proof drafts whose gaps all traced to overlap incoherence.
Source Rounds: 0

